Q: Trying to set up combo attack system in Unity2D under new input system. How do I reference a button being pressed? A: <p>I see a few issues in your code:</p>
<ol>
<li><p><strong>Mixing Input Systems:</strong> Mixing the old (<code>Input.GetKeyDown</code>) and the new (<code>InputAction.CallbackContext</code>) input systems is bad practice. It’s better to leave only the new one to avoid conflicts and sync issues.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>The Dead Timer:</strong> It’s not quite clear if you just didn't provide this code or if it's a bug, but in this code, <code>atkCountdown</code> never increases.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>One-shot Coroutines:</strong> Your <code>atkCancel</code> doesn't actually &quot;wait&quot; or loop. It checks the condition once and just dies immediately. You must use some cycle (while or something else).</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>Here is a working version (keeping your original structure and logic):</p>
<pre><code>public bool isSlashing = false;
private float atkCountdown = 0f;
private float atkCount;
private Coroutine cancelRoutine; // To reset the timer on each press

public void Slash(InputAction.CallbackContext context)
{
    if (context.started)
    {
        if (!isSlashing &amp;&amp; atkCountdown == 0f)
        {
            isSlashing = true;
            Debug.Log(&quot;slash!&quot;);

            PlayerAnimator.SetBool(&quot;Slashing&quot;, true);
            PlayerAnimator.SetFloat(&quot;SlashCount&quot;, 1);
        }
        else if (atkCountdown &lt;= 1f)
        {
            atkCount++;
            PlayerAnimator.SetFloat(&quot;SlashCount&quot;, atkCount);
            atkCountdown = 0f;
            Debug.Log(atkCount);

            if (atkCount &lt;= 6) { }
        }

        // Restart the cancel timer every time you press the button
        if (cancelRoutine != null) StopCoroutine(cancelRoutine);
        cancelRoutine = StartCoroutine(atkCancel());
    }
}

public IEnumerator atkCancel()
{
    // Using a loop so atkCountdown actually tracks time passing
    while (atkCountdown &lt; 1f)
    {
        atkCountdown += Time.deltaTime;
        yield return null;
    }

    // Logic runs only if the player didn't press the button again within 1s
    isSlashing = false;
    atkCountdown = 0f;
    atkCount = 0; 

    Debug.Log(&quot;Cancel time!&quot;);
    PlayerAnimator.SetBool(&quot;Slashing&quot;, false);
    PlayerAnimator.SetFloat(&quot;SlashCount&quot;, 0);
}
</code></pre>

Q: Count the number of rows with a particular column value using CodeIgniter A: <p>You miss <code>result()</code> or <code>result_array()</code>. You can try this, use this code as your model.</p>

<pre><code>function asia_count()
{
    $query = "SELECT COUNT(*) as Total FROM profiles WHERE location = 'Asia'";
    $result = $query-&gt;result_array();
    return $result[0]['Total'];
}
</code></pre>

<hr>

<p>If you want list all location count you can try this</p>

<p>Model</p>

<pre><code>function asia_count()
{
    $query = "SELECT location, COUNT(*) as Total FROM profiles GROUP BY location";
    return $query-&gt;result_array();
}
</code></pre>

<p>View</p>

<pre><code>foreach ($lists as $index =&gt; $row) {
    echo $row['location'] . ':' . $row['Total'];
}
</code></pre>

Q: Spring @Transactional on @Bean declaration instead of class Implementation A: <p>Found something built-in that is the sum of <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/1942666/mecon">@Mecon</a>'s and <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/1696044/erik-gillespie">@Erik Gillespie</a>'s answers, with limited boilerplate.</p>
<p>Spring already provides a <a href="http://docs.spring.io/spring/docs/current/javadoc-api/org/springframework/transaction/interceptor/TransactionProxyFactoryBean.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>TransactionProxyFactoryBean</code></a> that just sets up a transactional proxy on any object. Much of the setup could be refactored to some utility method :</p>
<pre><code>@Configuration
@EnableTransactionManagement
public class ComplexComponentConfig {

    /** NOT A @Bean, this object will be wrapped with a transactional proxy */
    public ComplexComponent complexComponentImpl() {
        return new ComplexComponentImpl(repository());
    }

    @Bean
    public TransactionProxyFactoryBean complexComponent() {
        TransactionProxyFactoryBean factory = new TransactionProxyFactoryBean();
        factory.setTarget(complexComponentImpl());
        factory.setTransactionAttributeSource(new MatchAlwaysTransactionAttributeSource());
        return factory;
    }

// ...
}
</code></pre>
